  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Antigua and Barbuda Employers&apos; Federation (ABEF) adapted its organizational structure and processes to respond more effectively to changes in the business environment and organizational needs. The ABEF amended its Handbook, which contains the Constitution and by-laws. The Constitution modification aimed to clarify the application process for membership and the procedures for suspension, resignation, and termination in case of bankruptcy. Additionally, it revised the by-laws, which sets out the internal rules by which the ABEF Governing Committee oversees the organization, and its vision and mission statements. The amendments to the governance structure were done to allow for faster and more efficient decision-making processes, and the revisions to the by-laws were done to strengthen the organization&apos;s charter and internal governance processes.
The amendments to the ABEF Handbook were presented to the Governing Committee on December 8th, 2020, and the ABEF Members approved them during an extraordinary meeting held on September 16th, 2021. The ILO developed the scope and methodology used by ABEF in the revision of their organizational structure and processes, and engaged a legal expert to lead the revision process.  

Additionally, the ILO organized several meetings with the legal expert and the ABEF President and Board Members in November 2020.  These meetings were intended to ensure that President and Board Members considered the new challenges the Federation was facing as a result of the impossibility of physical meetings, including the needs for voting mechanisms and reducing the Board members, in the revisions of the ABEF Constitution and by-laws, contained in the ABEF Handbook.

The amendments to the ABEF&apos;s organizational structures and processes were based on the ILO guidance to make more effective and efficient use of the ABEF&apos;s institutional capacities.
Furthermore, the ILO supported ABEF&#xB4;s capacity development by training two ABEF members in Management of Employers&#xB4; and Business Member Organizations through the Caribbean Academy jointly carried out by the ILO and the ITC-ILO. Additionally, the ILO trained one ABEF member to become a trainer on Essentials in Occupational Safety and Health in the Caribbean.</narrative>
   </description>
